"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,331\nEpisode 10\n\n3\n00:00:07,411 --> 00:00:09,869\nIt\u2019s all over by age three\n\n4\n00:00:12,669 --> 00:00:15,243\nThe first three years build the brain for life.\n\n5\n00:00:15,543 --> 00:00:17,748\nMiss that window, and the doors close.\n\n6\n00:00:17,940 --> 00:00:19,517\nIt is a powerful message.\n\n7\n00:00:19,725 --> 00:00:20,627\nIs it true?\n\n8\n00:00:20,796 --> 00:00:24,821\nIn the 1990s, this idea spread through reports,\n\n9\n00:00:25,089 --> 00:00:29,208\ncampaigns and a 1997 White House conference.\n\n10\n00:00:29,668 --> 00:00:34,834\nIn 1999, John Bruer examined its three pillars in his book,\n\n11\n00:00:35,085 --> 00:00:37,102\nThe Myth of the First Three Years.\n\n12\n00:00:37,272 --> 00:00:38,719\nFirst, synapses.\n\n13\n00:00:39,007 --> 00:00:42,201\nIt is true that connections form rapidly after birth.\n\n14\n00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:44,046\nBy about age three,\n\n15\n00:00:44,254 --> 00:00:48,841\nsynapse density peaks at levels roughly fifty percent higher than in adults,\n\n16\n00:00:49,079 --> 00:00:51,009\nand stays high until puberty.\n\n17\n00:00:51,498 --> 00:00:54,197\nBut more synapses does not mean more learning,\n\n18\n00:00:54,304 --> 00:00:57,921\nand learning continues as connections are pruned and refined.\n\n19\n00:00:58,091 --> 00:00:59,828\nSecond, critical periods.\n\n20\n00:01:00,158 --> 00:01:03,132\nThey are real for some sensory and motor systems.\n\n21\n00:01:03,532 --> 00:01:05,603\nVision is the classic example.\n\n22\n00:01:05,975 --> 00:01:10,358\nBut there is little evidence of such windows for most of what we learn in school,\n\n23\n00:01:10,469 --> 00:01:13,328\nlike reading, history or chemistry.\n\n24\n00:01:13,498 --> 00:01:15,659\nThird, enriched environments.\n\n25\n00:01:16,028 --> 00:01:18,350\nThe research was largely with rats,\n\n26\n00:01:18,499 --> 00:01:21,148\ncomparing barren cages to normal ones.\n\n27\n00:01:21,497 --> 00:01:25,448\nIt does not show that extra stimulation builds super brains.\n\n28\n00:01:25,618 --> 00:01:29,090\nThe grain of truth: early experience does matter,\n\n29\n00:01:29,212 --> 00:01:31,896\nand good early education helps many children.\n\n30\n00:01:32,312 --> 00:01:34,073\nBut the brain stays plastic.\n\n31\n00:01:34,536 --> 00:01:36,440\nAdults learn new languages,\n\n32\n00:01:36,474 --> 00:01:38,074\nskills and whole fields,\n\n33\n00:01:38,094 --> 00:01:39,925\nas we saw in Series one.\n\n34\n00:01:40,095 --> 00:01:41,230\nDo this instead.\n\n35\n00:01:41,637 --> 00:01:44,637\nTell students, especially returning and adult learners,\n\n36\n00:01:44,657 --> 00:01:46,224\nthat it is not too late.\n\n37\n00:01:46,561 --> 00:01:48,449\nBuild on what they already know.\n\n38\n00:01:48,758 --> 00:01:51,418\nAnd design for steady practice over time,\n\n39\n00:01:51,453 --> 00:01:53,064\nrather than a single window.\n\n40\n00:01:53,496 --> 00:01:57,981\nOne tip to share with students: it's never too late to learn something hard.\n\n41\n00:01:58,151 --> 00:02:00,173\nThe early years open doors.\n\n42\n00:02:00,351 --> 00:02:02,048\nThey do not close the others.\n\n"